---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
import contrastTesting from '../samples/wcag-143-testing.txt?raw';
import statusMethod from '../samples/wcag-413-method.json.txt?raw';
import summary from '../samples/wcag-summary.txt?raw';

const toc = [
   { id: 'methods', label: 'Three test methods' },
   { id: 'numbers', label: 'The numbers' },
   { id: 'one', label: 'How to test one criterion' },
   { id: 'data', label: 'The method as data' },
   { id: 'claims', label: 'What you can claim' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test methods"
   summary="<p>WCAG has 86 success criteria. A scanner cannot decide a single one of them alone. a11ied labels each criterion with how it has to be tested, so you know what to test after a scan.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="methods">Three test methods</h2>
      <p>
         WCAG, the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, states its requirements as numbered
         success criteria, such as 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum). a11ied gives each one a test
         method.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>automated</code></dt>
         <dd>A tool decides pass or fail alone. No criterion in WCAG 2.2 has this method.</dd>
         <dt><code>hybrid</code></dt>
         <dd>A tool produces evidence and a person judges it. The evidence is a scan result for part of the criterion, or a screen reader transcript. Example: 1.4.3 Contrast. axe measures the text it can, and a person checks text over images and gradients.</dd>
         <dt><code>manual</code></dt>
         <dd>A person reads and decides. Example: 1.3.3 Sensory Characteristics asks whether instructions rely on shape or position alone. No tool measures that.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Reports print these in plain words: "scan and a person" for hybrid, "a person" for
         manual. The person can be an AI agent that has the criterion and the evidence. What
         matters is that something judges.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="numbers">The numbers</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag criteria --summary</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{summary.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Meeting Level AA, the level most policies require, means meeting Levels A and AA
         together: 55 criteria, 29 hybrid and 26 manual. axe
         has a rule for 28 of the 86. A rule covers part of its criterion. Passing every rule
         means no rule found a failure, and each criterion still needs its other part
         checked.
      </p>
      <p>
         The methods come from the WCAG tags on axe-core's rules and from the W3C's
         <abbr title="Accessibility Conformance Testing">ACT</abbr> rules. Where neither has a
         rule, a11ied assigns the method itself. Each record says which under
         <code>sourceAttribution</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="one">How to test one criterion</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 wcag &lt;criterion&gt;</code> prints the requirement, then a
         <strong>Testing it</strong> section: what a scan covers, what to do by hand, and
         what counts as a pass or a fail.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 1.4.3</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{contrastTesting.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         When you do not know the number, search for the behavior:
         <code>a1 search "keyboard trap"</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="data">The method as data</h2>
      <p>With <code>--json</code>, a script or an agent gets the same answer as two objects.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{statusMethod.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>testMethod.method</code></dt>
         <dd><code>hybrid</code> or <code>manual</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>axeRuleIds</code>, <code>actRuleIds</code></dt>
         <dd>The rules that cover part of the criterion. Both are empty here: no rule tests status messages.</dd>
         <dt><code>strategy.requiresRealTarget</code></dt>
         <dd><code>true</code> when the test needs a running page, because markup alone cannot show it. A status message has to be triggered and heard.</dd>
         <dt><code>strategy.procedureIds</code></dt>
         <dd>The named procedure that <strong>Testing it</strong> prints.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="claims">What you can claim</h2>
      <p>For each statement you make, say which kind of evidence supports it.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>A scan</dt>
         <dd>"No axe rule failed on this page." It does not support "this page meets WCAG".</dd>
         <dt>A transcript</dt>
         <dd>"The screen reader announced the dialog and its name." Say which screen reader, and whether it was simulated.</dd>
         <dt>A judgment</dt>
         <dd>"The alt text describes the image." Say who decided, and on what.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <a href="/guides/audit"><code>a1 audit</code></a> records all three for every
         criterion and writes them into a report.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
